.SS Abstract
This header defines macros that remove padding bytes from structures,
reducing their memory footprint.
.PP
Do not use this by default.
Packed structures should be limited to cases where the memory layout
must be exact (binary formats, network protocols, file I/O).
Removing padding can introduce unaligned memory access and measurable
performance penalties.
.PP
Before the solution, the problem:
.PP
CPUs access memory more efficiently when data is aligned to natural
boundaries (typically 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 bytes).
To satisfy these requirements, compilers automatically insert padding
bytes into structures.
.PP
\f[B]Examples\f[R]:
.PP
A single \f[CR]int\f[R] is naturally aligned\-no padding needed:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] test
{
    int a;
}; \f[I]// sizeof = 4 bytes\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Padding is inserted so the structure size remains aligned:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] test
{
    int a;  \f[I]// 4\f[R]
    char b; \f[I]// 1 + 3 (4)\f[R]
}; \f[I]// sizeof = 8 bytes (3 bytes padding)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Pointer alignment forces additional padding:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] test
{
    void *a; \f[I]// 8\f[R]
    char b;  \f[I]// 1 + 7 (8)\f[R]
}; \f[I]// sizeof = 16 bytes (7 bytes padding)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Trailing members are padded to preserve alignment rules:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] test
{
    void *a; \f[I]// 8\f[R]
    void *b; \f[I]// 8\f[R]
    short c; \f[I]// 2 + 6 (8)\f[R]
}; \f[I]// sizeof = 24 bytes (6 bytes padding)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Large alignment requirements can waste significant space:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] test
{
    long double a; \f[I]// 16\f[R]
    char b;        \f[I]// 1 + 15 (16)\f[R]
}; \f[I]// sizeof = 32 bytes (15 bytes padding... What a waste)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Padding exists to keep memory accesses aligned and fast.
.PP
If the structure is packed:
.IP
.EX
PRAGMA_PACK_PUSH
\f[B]struct\f[R] test
{
    int a;
    char b;
} PACK;
PRAGMA_PACK_POP
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]sizeof(struct test)\f[R] becomes \f[B]5 bytes\f[R].
.PP
This eliminates padding but may cause unaligned accesses, which can be
slower or even fault on some architectures.
.PP
Sharing packed structures across APIs, modules, or hot paths is a common
source of unnecessary performance loss.
Use them deliberately, not habitually.
